    "atom::e1f1f9e8dbb36c4251377288": {
      "atom_id": "atom::e1f1f9e8dbb36c4251377288",
      "source_handle": "P0212",
      "field_path": [
        "supplement_materials"
      ],
      "value": [
        {
          "auxiliary_material": "The study provides detailed safety data (42% grade 1–2 adverse events, 37% grade 3–4 adverse events) and mechanistic hypotheses (e.g., increasing beneficial bacteria like Akkermansia muciniphila, modulating tumor microenvironment) which, while not validating the Phase III gap, offer context on why such trials are being pursued. It also notes heterogeneity in FMT protocols (donor sources, administration routes) as a barrier that large-scale trials need to standardize.",
          "evidence": [],
          "gap_id": "phase_iii_rct_evidence",
          "judgment_path": "F:\\OptoMind-Review-2\\outputs\\body_full_staged_acceptance_20261004_40cny\\planning\\level2\\external\\N48436b3aa73aea86f35f-f45b87be8e30256e\\round_1\\supplements\\phase_iii_rct_evidence\\attempt-c7j_i2fn\\source_units\\SU01_search_candidate_d1c4b32e8c\\FULFILLMENT_JUDGMENT.json",
          "limitations": [],
          "material_ready": true,
          "material_usefulness": "supporting",
          "outline_action": "Use this meta-analysis to substantiate the claim that current evidence is limited to early-phase (I/II) and small-scale RCTs. Cite the authors' conclusion regarding the necessity of large-scale Phase III trials to justify the gap. Do not present the 3 included RCTs as fulfilling the 'large-scale Phase III' criterion, as the study itself classifies them as limited and calls for further validation.",
          "outline_action_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "remaining_gap": "The specific evidence requested—completed large-scale randomized controlled Phase III clinical trials confirming efficacy—is not present in the literature covered by this study. The 164 patients across 10 studies do not constitute a large-scale Phase III dataset, and the authors explicitly state the need for such trials to validate efficacy and safety.",
          "remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "status": "unmet",
          "useful_material": "This study is a systematic review and meta-analysis (search up to September 30, 2024) that included 10 studies involving 164 patients with solid tumors treated with FMT combined with ICIs. The authors report that the included studies comprised 5 phase I trials, 4 phase II–III trials, and 3 randomized controlled trials (RCTs), but note that most were early-phase clinical trials with small sample sizes. The pooled objective response rate (ORR) was 43%, with subgroup analysis showing higher ORR (60%) for anti-PD-1 combined with anti-CTLA-4 therapy compared to monotherapy (37%). Crucially, the authors conclude in both the Abstract and Discussion that 'larger-scale randomized controlled trials with long-term follow-up are required to confirm and optimize treatment protocols' and highlight the 'absence of randomized controlled trials' (referring to large-scale/definitive ones) as a limitation. This material directly addresses the gap question by confirming that while preliminary RCT data exists, completed large-scale Phase III evidence is currently lacking and identified as a necessary future step."
        }
      ],
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::153738efad1ff78582447def2bcb3c312f0a586a7520f897f63871d450a16bd8",
          "field_path": [
